---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Base
  title="ExCSV formats"
  description="Five ways to ship the same ExCSV: a single file, a sidecar next to plain CSV, a peekable ZIP, a columnar multi-table pack, or JSON."
  wide
>
  <article class="formats">
    <h1>Formats</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      ExCSV is one language — types, units, stats, SQL — sitting on top of CSV.
      Four of the variants are just packaging: how that language travels with the rows.
      The fifth, JSON, is the same document in a different serialization.
      Pick by whether you can touch the CSV, how big the file is, whether you're shipping
      one table or a snapshot of several — and whether the consumer speaks CSV or JSON.
    </p>

    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Formats">
      <a href="#plain">Plain</a>
      <a href="#sidecar">Sidecar</a>
      <a href="#zip">ZIP</a>
      <a href="#pack">Pack</a>
      <a href="#json">JSON</a>
    </nav>

    <div class="pick-grid">
      <a class="pick" href="#plain">
        <span class="pick-ext">.excsv</span>
        <h2>Plain</h2>
        <p>One file. Schema and rows together. The default.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#sidecar">
        <span class="pick-ext">.excsv + .csv</span>
        <h2>Sidecar</h2>
        <p>Annotate a CSV you can't — or won't — modify.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#zip">
        <span class="pick-ext">.excsv.zip</span>
        <h2>ZIP</h2>
        <p>Compress a table. Peek schema and sums without unzipping.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#pack">
        <span class="pick-ext">.excsv.pack.zip</span>
        <h2>Pack</h2>
        <p>Wide tables and multi-table snapshots, column by column.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#json">
        <span class="pick-ext">.excsv.json</span>
        <h2>JSON</h2>
        <p>The same document as JSON, for APIs and AI output.</p>
      </a>
    </div>

    <h2 id="pick">Which one</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>You have…</th>
            <th>Use</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A table you produce</td>
            <td><a href="#plain">Plain</a></td>
            <td>One artifact. Diff it, email it, <code>pd.read_csv(..., comment="#")</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A CSV you must not change</td>
            <td><a href="#sidecar">Sidecar</a></td>
            <td>Original bytes stay. Excel / Tableau still open <code>sales.csv</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A large table to send</td>
            <td><a href="#zip">ZIP</a></td>
            <td>Smaller. Schema and aggregations sit in the ZIP comment — preview before extract.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A wide table, or several related ones</td>
            <td><a href="#pack">Pack</a></td>
            <td>Read <code>amount</code> without loading <code>note</code>. Ship orders + customers as one file.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>An API, a config file, or an LLM writing the table</td>
            <td><a href="#json">JSON</a></td>
            <td>Real types, one JSON Schema to validate against. Round-trips back to CSV.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <section class="form" id="plain">
      <p class="kicker">Default</p>
      <h2>Plain — one file</h2>
      <p>
        Header, <code>#</code> lines, then ordinary CSV rows, all in <code>sales.excsv</code>
        (or <code>sales.extsv</code> if the delimiter is a tab). This is the canonical form:
        what you export, version, paste into a chat, or drop next to a notebook.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every CSV reader that ignores <code>#</code> comments already reads the data.
        pandas: <code>pd.read_csv("sales.excsv", comment="#")</code>.
        <code>grep</code> / <code>awk</code> / <code>cut</code> work on the rows as usual.
      </p>

      <div class="pros">
        <h3>Why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One artifact.</strong> No pair to keep together, no archive to unpack.</li>
          <li><strong>Readable diffs.</strong> Git sees the schema change and the row change in the same file.</li>
          <li><strong>CLI-native.</strong> Meta lines start with <code>#</code>, so Unix tools skip them for free.</li>
          <li><strong>Pasteable.</strong> Drop a snippet into Slack or an LLM — types and totals come with it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <CodeBlock title="sales.excsv" description="Schema and rows in one place.">
{`#!excsv version=0.4 delim=comma header=1
#@grain: one row per order
#column name=id type=int role=id
#column name=customer type=string
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD role=measure
#%sum: ,,750.50
id,customer,amount
1,Acme Corp,500.00
2,Globex Inc,250.50`}
      </CodeBlock>

      <p class="skip">If the CSV already exists and you can't rewrite it — that's sidecar.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="form" id="sidecar">
      <p class="kicker">Legacy-friendly</p>
      <h2>Sidecar — annotate without touching the CSV</h2>
      <p>
        <code>sales.csv</code> stays a normal CSV — same bytes, same checksum, still opens in Excel.
        Next to it, <code>sales.excsv</code> holds only metadata and points at the data with
        <code>reference=sales.csv</code>. No rows in the sidecar. Tabs use
        <code>sales.extsv</code> + <code>sales.tsv</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You open whichever you have. Opening the CSV, an ExCSV-aware tool looks for the sibling.
        Opening the sidecar, it follows <code>reference=</code>. Everyone else just reads the CSV.
      </p>

      <div class="pros">
        <h3>Why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Don't touch the source.</strong> Vendor dumps, regulated extracts, lake files with a hash someone else checks.</li>
          <li><strong>Existing tools keep working.</strong> Tableau, Excel, a random <code>COPY</code> script — they see a CSV, not a new format.</li>
          <li><strong>Several views of the same data.</strong> Different sidecars can describe the same file for different audiences.</li>
          <li><strong>Stats describe the CSV, not the sidecar.</strong> Row count, checksum, <code>#%</code> totals are about the referenced file.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="pair">
        <CodeBlock title="sales.excsv" description="Metadata only — no data rows.">
{`#!excsv version=0.4 delim=comma header=1 rows=2 reference=sales.csv
#@source: sales_db.orders
#column name=id type=int
#column name=customer type=string
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD
#%sum: ,,750.50`}
        </CodeBlock>
        <CodeBlock title="sales.csv" description="Ordinary CSV. Unchanged.">
{`id,customer,amount
1,Acme Corp,500.00
2,Globex Inc,250.50`}
        </CodeBlock>
      </div>

      <p class="skip">If you control the export, a plain <code>.excsv</code> is enough.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="form" id="zip">
      <p class="kicker">Distribution</p>
      <h2>ZIP — compress, still peekable</h2>
      <p>
        Wrap a plain file as <code>sales.excsv.zip</code> (or <code>.extsv.zip</code>).
        Inside: the same inline ExCSV. Outside: a normal ZIP that anything can open.
        pandas will take it with <code>pd.read_csv("sales.excsv.zip", comment="#")</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trick is the ZIP comment. It repeats the header, columns, and <code>#%</code> aggregations,
        so you can see schema and totals <em>without extracting</em> — useful when a folder is full of
        200&nbsp;MB exports and you need to know which one is Q1 sales.
      </p>

      <div class="pros">
        <h3>Why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Smaller to send.</strong> Email, S3, Slack — same table, less bytes.</li>
          <li><strong>Preview before unzip.</strong> Schema, grain, sums in the archive comment.</li>
          <li><strong>Still a ZIP.</strong> No custom container. Password-protect it the usual way if you need to.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <pre><code>sales.excsv.zip
└── sales.excsv          ← ordinary plain file (header + rows)</code></pre>

      <p class="skip">
        Stay on plain if you want <code>zcat | awk</code> without an archive.
        Several tables is pack. A sidecar stays two files on disk — ZIP is for one plain file.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="form" id="pack">
      <p class="kicker">Columnar · multi-table</p>
      <h2>Pack — columns and several tables</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>.excsv.pack.zip</code> is a small warehouse dump. Instead of storing rows,
        it stores each <strong>column</strong> as its own file, and can hold <strong>several tables</strong>
        plus the foreign keys between them. Same ExCSV metadata — types, stats, SQL — per table.
      </p>
      <p>
        Think: you exported orders, customers, and products, and you usually only need
        <code>amount</code> and <code>date</code> out of a 200-column fact table.
        A row ZIP makes you decompress everything. A pack lets you open one <code>.col</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="pros">
        <h3>Why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Selective reads.</strong> Pull two columns from a wide table without paying for the rest.</li>
          <li><strong>Better compression.</strong> A file of one type (IDs, amounts, dates) squeezes harder than mixed rows.</li>
          <li><strong>A snapshot, not a sheet.</strong> Related tables and their FKs travel as one file.</li>
          <li><strong>Cheap extra column.</strong> Add a <code>.col</code>, rewrite that table's header. Don't rebuild the whole dump.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <pre><code>sales.excsv.pack.zip
├── _manifest.excsv          ← tables + foreign keys
├── orders/
│   ├── _header.excsv        ← schema + stats for this table
│   ├── 00-id.col
│   ├── 01-customer_id.col
│   └── 02-amount.col
├── customers/
│   └── ...
└── products/
    └── ...</code></pre>

      <p>
        Wide fact tables can split each column into row chunks (default 10k) so a single cell
        is a short scan, not a full-column read. Small lookup tables stay as one file per column.
      </p>
      <p class="skip">
        Stay on plain or ZIP if you want to stream rows. Pack is in v0.4;
        the <a href="/tools/">Go CLI</a> doesn't open it yet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="form" id="json">
      <p class="kicker">JSON-native</p>
      <h2>JSON — the same document, without the CSV</h2>
      <p>
        <code>sales.excsv.json</code> is not an export or a summary — it's the same ExCSV document
        with the <code>#</code> stripped off. Every meta line becomes a key: the header is
        <code>csv</code>, <code>#@</code> is <code>meta</code>, <code>#column</code> is
        <code>columns</code>, <code>#%</code> is <code>aggregates</code>, <code>#$</code> is <code>sql</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use it where JSON is already the envelope: a REST payload, a config file, a TypeScript frontend —
        or an LLM that you'd rather constrain to a
        <a href="/schema/excsv-0.4.schema.json">JSON Schema</a> than trust to hand-write a
        <code>#!excsv</code> header. Serve it as <code>application/excsv+json</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="pros">
        <h3>Why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Real types.</strong> <code>required=1</code> is <code>true</code>, <code>enum=a|b|c</code> is an array, an empty cell is <code>null</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Validation for free.</strong> One JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) checks the whole vocabulary — in your editor, in CI, or as an LLM response format.</li>
          <li><strong>Money survives.</strong> <code>decimal</code> and <code>long</code> cells are written as strings, so <code>500.00</code> never becomes a float.</li>
          <li><strong>Lossless both ways.</strong> CSV → JSON → CSV gives you the same file back. The text form stays canonical; only free-text <code>##</code> comments drop.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <CodeBlock title="sales.excsv.json" description="The plain example above, as JSON.">
{`{
  "excsv": "0.4",
  "csv": { "delim": "comma", "header": true },
  "meta": { "grain": "one row per order" },
  "columns": [
    { "index": 0, "name": "id", "type": "int", "role": "id" },
    { "index": 1, "name": "customer", "type": "string" },
    { "index": 2, "name": "amount", "type": "decimal", "unit": "USD", "role": "measure" }
  ],
  "aggregates": { "sum": [null, null, "750.50"] },
  "rows": 2,
  "data": [
    [1, "Acme Corp", "500.00"],
    [2, "Globex Inc", "250.50"]
  ]
}`}
      </CodeBlock>

      <p class="skip">
        It's JSON, not CSV — no <code>#</code> comment trick, no <code>awk</code> on the rows, no ZIP-comment preview.
        Stay on plain when plain-CSV compatibility is the point.
        <a href="/convert/">Try the browser converter</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="more">
      More of the file language on the <a href="/spec/">guide</a>.
      Concrete files on <a href="/examples/">Examples</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .formats h1 {
    margin-top: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .jump {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .jump a {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.35em 0.75em;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .jump a:hover {
    color: var(--color-text);
    border-color: var(--color-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .pick-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .pick {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1.15rem 1.2rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .pick:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .pick-ext {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: 0.55rem;
  }
  .pick h2 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .pick p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .table-wrap table {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .form {
    padding-top: 2rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 4.5rem;
  }
  .form > h2 {
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
  }
  .kicker {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .pros {
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1.15rem 1.35rem 0.85rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .pros h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .pros ul {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .pros li {
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .skip {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .pick-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pick-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .pick-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
